# Environment: OpenCode native on Termux/Android

_This section is installed and kept in sync by
[opencode-code-termux-native](https://github.com/jronminh/opencode-code-termux-native)'s
`install.sh`, and is wired into your global `~/.config/opencode/opencode.jsonc`
via `instructions` so opencode reads it at every session start. A future
install/update overwrites everything between the
`opencode-code-termux-native:begin`/`:end` markers, so don't hand-edit inside
them — edit `environment.md.template` in that repo instead, then re-run
`install.sh`._

You are NOT running on a regular Linux system. This opencode is the aarch64
build published by the [C04-wq/opencode-termux](https://github.com/C04-wq/opencode-termux)
community project: a Bun-based binary linked against **musl**, whose ELF
interpreter is patched by this repo's `install.sh` to the musl loader at
`~/.opencode/ld-musl-aarch64.so.1`. A real `android-arm64`/Bionic build is not
published; this is a community workaround, not official support. Consequence:
`process.platform` may report `linux` or `android` — don't branch any important
decision on that alone.

**The launcher matters.** `$PREFIX/bin/opencode` is a wrapper this repo
installs that `exec`s the loader explicitly:

    exec "$HOME/.opencode/ld-musl-aarch64.so.1" \
      --library-path "$HOME/.opencode" \
      "$HOME/.opencode/opencode" "$@"

That `--library-path` form is **mandatory** for this musl build (direct exec
fails to find `libstdc++.so.6`), and it scopes the library search to that one
exec — nothing leaks into children. It replaces the stock npm launcher, which
exported `LD_PRELOAD`/`LD_LIBRARY_PATH` env vars that crashed/hung the Bionic
bash and ripgrep that opencode itself spawns. Never re-export either var, and
never hand-edit the wrapper or anything under `~/.opencode/opencode-native/`.

If opencode, grep, bash, find, or an update misbehaves — segfault on launch,
"invalid ELF header", "cannot execute", grep/`-G` shared-library errors,
bash/ripgrep hanging or dying mid-tool-call, `LD_PRELOAD`/`LD_LIBRARY_PATH`
weirdness, `patchelf`/loader questions — the fix is to re-run `install.sh`
(which re-downloads, re-patches, and re-wires everything idempotently).
Update deliberately with `termux-update-opencode`; the in-process autoupdater
is disabled on purpose (it would drop in an unpatched, unrunnable build).

You also have Android-native `termux-api` bridges available directly as shell
commands (needs the Termux:API app installed and granted —
`command -v termux-notification` is a quick way to check):

- **`termux-open <path-or-url>`** — opens a file or URL in whatever Android
  app is the default handler (PDF viewer, image viewer, browser). Use it when
  the user asks to view/open something you just produced, rather than just
  naming the path.
- **`termux-share <path>`** — opens Android's native share sheet for a file,
  so the user can send it to another app without leaving Termux. Use it when
  the user asks to share/send a file somewhere.
- Both are fire-and-forget: no output to parse, just call them and mention
  what you did.
- If the battery is low and not charging (`termux-battery-status` reports
  `"status": "DISCHARGING"`), prefer batching work over starting long
  unattended background tasks until it's charging.
